var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"The works cited in these docs and docstrings.\n\nFörstner, W. and Gülch, E. (1987). A Fast Operator for Detection and Precise Location of Distinct Points, Corners and Centres of Circular Features. In: Proceedings of the ISPRS Intercommission Conference on Fast Processing of Photogrammetric Data (Interlaken, Switzerland); pp. 281–305.\n\n\n\nKöthe, U. (2003). Edge and Junction Detection with an Improved Structure Tensor. In: Pattern Recognition, 25th DAGM Symposium, Vol. 2781 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science (Springer, Berlin, Heidelberg); pp. 25–32.\n\n\n\nLuxen, M. (2005). Interest Point Detector by Förstner/Gülch (Förstner-Operator), MATLAB implementation ip_fop.m, version 1.0. Department of Photogrammetry, Institute of Geodesy and Geoinformation, University of Bonn; contact Thomas Läbe. Dated 13.01.2005, with changes from 10.03.2011.\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#The-Förstner-operator","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"detect_foerstner is the interest operator of Förstner and Gülch (Förstner and Gülch, 1987): it finds windows of a grey image whose gradients locate a point well, and estimates in each a junction point and a circular point by least squares, keeping the one a significance test prefers. It follows the MATLAB reference implementation ip_fop.m (Luxen, 2005) step by step, including the two extensions it takes from Köthe (Köthe, 2003): the image is processed at doubled resolution, and the :koethe window criterion ranks windows by the smaller eigenvalue of the structure tensor.\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\npoints  = detect_foerstner(img)                          # Float32, default_device()\ncorners = filter(d -> d.label === :corner, points)\nEllipse(first(corners); sigma = 1)                       # the 1σ error ellipse (ip_errell.m)","title":"The Förstner operator"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#What-it-returns","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"One row per window, a StructArray of Detection(Uncertain(PointFrame(x), Σ), (; label, window, F)). The frame is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's PointFrame, a position without a scale: neither point model estimates a size.\n\nlabel the row's point and covariance\n:corner the junction point (the reference's corner)\n:circular the reference's circular point (dot_point): the centre of a rotationally symmetric pattern, a dot or a ring. Förstner & Gülch's term; it carries no size estimate and is not a scale-space blob\n:unknown the window centre and a NaN covariance: the significance test labelled neither (the reference's noclass, which has no estimate)\n\nwindow is the window centre (the reference's win), and F the statistic Ω_corner / Ω_circular the label was decided on.","title":"What it returns"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Coordinates","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"Positions are COLMAP pixel centres of the input image, (x, y) = (column, row), the top-left pixel's centre at (0.5, 0.5), as every detector of the package returns them. MATLAB indexes the pixel (r, c) at its centre, so a position (r, c) of the reference is (c − ½, r − ½) here.\n\nThe operator works on the doubled image. The package's one 2× upsampling (the ASIFT seed's, VLFeat's grid) makes the doubled pixel (r′, c′) (1-based) the bilinear sample at input position (r′/2, c′/2), 1-based, so a doubled-grid position is at COLMAP ((c′ − 1)/2, (r′ − 1)/2) in the input, and a covariance shrinks by 4. The conversion is made once, at the output.","title":"Coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Devices-and-precision","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"The image-wide steps — the doubling, the gradient and its integration, the window measures, the regional maxima and the non-maximum suppression, and the two least-squares estimates per window — are KernelAbstractions kernels, the same code on KA.CPU() and on a GPU (device = CUDABackend()). The F-test and the result are assembled on the host. T sets the precision: Float32 by default, Float64 as the reference. On the reference's example image Quadrate.tif (500 × 500, 100 windows) a CUDA device and the CPU give the same labels and positions within 3.1 × 10⁻⁵ px in Float32 and 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁴ px in Float64; the test suite checks it whenever a CUDA device is present.","title":"Devices and precision"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Accuracy","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"On area-sampled synthetic dots (radius 2.5 px, sub-pixel centres spread over the whole frame up to 8 px from every border) every circular point comes back within 0.0054 px of the truth. A junction is less exact: at an X-junction the window measure is flat around the vertex, so the window can land a few doubled pixels off it, and the point estimate in an off-centre window is biased by up to ≈ 0.1 px (within its reported covariance); this is the operator's, not a difference from the reference.\n\nOn Quadrate.tif with the defaults, both window criteria find 100 windows, all labelled :corner; with the reference example's parameters (noise_sigma = 1, integration = :gaussian, derivative_sigma = 0.7, integration_sigma = 2) also 100 corners, median position standard deviation 0.057 px.","title":"Accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Differences-from-ip_fop.m","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"The implementation follows the reference line for line except in three places, each recorded here with its evidence.","title":"Differences from ip_fop.m"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#1.-Undoing-the-doubling:-fixed","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"The reference doubles the image with imresize(g, 2, 'bilinear') (line 172) and maps positions back with transf_H, transf_v (lines 340–350, applied at 436, 446 and 457): x = (1 − n)/(1 − 2n)·x′ + (1 − 2)n/(1 − 2n) per axis, n the image size. That map is the inverse of an align-corners grid, on which the doubled pixels 1 and 2n sit on the input pixels 1 and n. imresize's grid is not that: its doubled pixel j samples the input at j/2 + 1/4 (1-based), so the exact inverse is x = x′/2 + 1/4, and the reference's points are off by ((n + 1)/2 − x)/(2n − 1): zero at the image centre, up to 0.25 px toward the centre at the borders.\n\nWhat this rests on, since MATLAB cannot be run here: MATLAB's imresize documentation states that antialiasing applies only when shrinking, so a 2× bilinear enlargement is plain bilinear interpolation; and imresize.m maps output to input coordinates by u = x/scale + 0.5·(1 − 1/scale), i.e. u = x/2 + 1/4 for scale = 2, with the out-of-range neighbour of the first sample taken from the edge pixel (index mirroring, which at this offset replicates the border). The tests emulate that grid to measure the reference's map.\n\nMeasured on 30–42 area-sampled dots spread over the frame (sub-pixel centres, up to 8 px from every border), sampled on imresize's grid:\n\nback-map largest error\nthe reference's transf_H, transf_v 0.236 px (borders; ≈ 0 at the centre), matching the predicted ((n + 1)/2 − x)/(2n − 1) to 0.002 px at every dot\nimresize's exact inverse 0.0054 px\nthe package's upsampling grid, its exact inverse (what detect_foerstner does) 0.0054 px\n\nThe package's grid (input j/2) is a quarter input pixel from imresize's (input j/2 + 1/4); the doubled samples differ accordingly, and the measured effect on the positions is nil at this precision (0.0054 px either way).","title":"1. Undoing the doubling: fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#2.-The-circular-point's-residual-sum:-kept-as-the-reference-has-it","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"Line 423 computes the circular point's weighted residual sum as Σ γ_cc·Δr_dot² − 2γ_rc·Δr_corner·Δc_corner + γ_rr·Δc_corner²: two of its three terms use the junction model's residuals. Förstner and Gülch's circular model has, for each pixel i, the residual ∇gᵢ⊥ᵀ(x̂ − xᵢ) (the distance of the point from the line through the pixel along its gradient, weighted by the gradient), so its residual sum is Σ (xᵢ − x̂)ᵀ Γᵢ⊥ (xᵢ − x̂) with all three terms from the circular estimate. That sum enters the covariance Ω/redundancy · N⁻¹ (line 449) and the test statistic (line 427).\n\nA Monte Carlo test of the reported covariance does not support the derived form. On noisy synthetic dots (radius 2.5 px, random sub-pixel centres, 400 trials each; NEES eᵀΣ⁻¹e, nominal mean 2):\n\nnoise (of the [0, 1] range)  mean NEES 95 % coverage 99.9 % coverage\n0.01 line 423 (kept) 3.0 0.85 0.965\n0.01 derived residual sum 13.7 0.375 0.65\n0.03 line 423 (kept) 8.7 0.578 0.805\n0.03 derived residual sum 20.4 0.315 0.52\n\nNeither form is calibrated for circular points (both under-cover: the variance factor treats the gradients of a window as independent, which the smoothing makes them not), and the reference's form is the closer one. The labels were the same under both in all 1 600 trials (dots and X-junctions at both noise levels), the junction covariance does not involve it (NEES 0.91 and 1.72, 95 % coverage 0.998 and 0.96), and the reference's sum was never ≤ 0. So line 423 is kept as written; a test item reruns a 100-trial version of the comparison.","title":"2. The circular point's residual sum: kept as the reference has it"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#3.-Defaults:-the-code's","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"The reference's header documents SIGMA_N = 2.5 grey values and SIGMA_DIFF = 0.7 px (lines 37 and 50); its code runs with 2.0 and 1.0 (lines 521 and 524). The defaults here are the code's — what ip_fop.m actually ran. This is a conflict in the reference's documentation, not a correction.","title":"3. Defaults: the code's"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foerstner/#Also-as-the-reference","page":"The Förstner operator","text":"noise_sigma is in grey levels of an 8-bit image, as the reference's for a uint8 image: the operator reads the image in [0, 1] and uses noise_sigma / 255. The reference stretches a floating-point image to its own range; this does not. With a Gaussian integration the estimation window still weighs its pixels equally, which the reference's header flags as an inconsistency.\n\nThe full docstring: detect_foerstner.","title":"Also as the reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cache/#Cache-and-Visualization","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"FileCache needs the JLD2 extension (using JLD2), and detections! needs a Makie backend.","title":"Cache and Visualization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.FileCache","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"FileCache{D}\n\nWrapper around an open file-backed key-value store. The concrete backend D is determined by the extension (e.g., JLD2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.FileCache"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cache_key-Tuple","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"cache_key(args...) -> String\n\nCompute a stable hex-encoded SHA-256 cache key from arbitrary arguments. The caller decides what goes in; this function just hashes.\n\nExample\n\nkey = cache_key(board_uid, model_hash, cutoff, patch_size)\nget!(cache, key) do ... end\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cache_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.close_cache-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"close_cache(cache)\n\nClose the cache file handle. No-op for nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.close_cache"},{"category":"method","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.open_cache-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"open_cache(path) -> FileCache or nothing\n\nOpen a persistent cache file. Returns nothing if path is nothing or the file cannot be opened (logs a warning).\n\nRequires a backend extension (e.g., using JLD2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.open_cache"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cache/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detections!","page":"Cache and Visualization","text":"detections!(ax, dets::AbstractVector{<:Detection};\n            cutoff=1.0, color_by=nothing, colormap=:viridis, kwargs...)\n\nOverlay detections as batched ellipse boundaries (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's ellipses!).\n\ncolor_by selects the stroke coloring:\n\nnothing — constant strokecolor\n:response — |metadata.response| through colormap\nan AbstractVector{<:Real} — explicit per-detection values\n\nRequires Makie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detections!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#Matching","page":"Matching","text":"","title":"Matching"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#Nearest-neighbour-matching","page":"Matching","text":"nn_match and match compare two descriptor matrices, one descriptor per row, and return a Vector{NNMatch} sorted best first. Both run the same KernelAbstractions kernels: match on the CPU, nn_match on any device (device = CUDABackend(), for instance), and a GPU returns what the CPU returns. match is public but not exported, because it would shadow Base.match; call it as VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match.\n\nThe strategy is a positional argument:\n\nMutualNN (the default) keeps pairs that are each other's nearest neighbour;\nNN keeps every row's nearest neighbour;\nKnnMatch(k) keeps every row's k nearest neighbours, for a downstream geometric check to disambiguate.\n\nThe metric is a keyword: CosineSimilarity (the default, for unit descriptors) or L2. ratio_threshold applies Lowe's ratio test, and min_similarity or max_distance bound the score.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> D1 = Float32[1 0; 0 1; 0.6 0.8];\n\njulia> D2 = Float32[0 1; 1 0];\n\njulia> [(m.first, m.second) for m in nn_match(D1, D2)]\n2-element Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}:\n (1, 2)\n (2, 1)\n\njulia> length(nn_match(D1, D2, NN()))    # row 3 matches too, one way\n3","title":"Nearest-neighbour matching"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#Matches-as-correspondences","page":"Matching","text":"An NNMatch holds indices. csponds(features1, features2, matches) turns them into VisualGeometryToolkitCore correspondences between the matched features, with each match's score and ratio as attribute columns, ready for a robust estimator.\n\nDescribedFeature pairs a detection with its descriptor. described_features builds a StructArray of them from detections and a descriptor matrix, and descriptor_matrix returns the descriptors as a matrix without copying; match and csponds accept the StructArray directly.","title":"Matches as correspondences"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#Many-galleries-at-once","page":"Matching","text":"nn_match_stacked matches one set of features against many galleries (a dictionary of descriptor matrices) in one matrix product, with the same result per gallery as matching that gallery alone. A StackedDescriptorIndex keeps the stacked galleries on the device between calls.","title":"Many galleries at once"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/matching/#LoMa","page":"Matching","text":"LoMaDescriptor and LoMaMatcher run the learned DeDoDe descriptors and LoMa matchers of Nordström et al. (ECCV 2026) from the authors' ONNX exports, through ONNX Runtime on the CPU or CUDA. The models are downloaded on first use and cached in the package's scratch space. A descriptor describes the strongest detections of an image; a matcher matches two described sets and returns Vector{NNMatch}, like match:\n\ndescribe = LoMaDescriptor(:b)\nmatcher = LoMaMatcher(:b128)\nfa = describe(img_a, asift_detect(img_a))\nfb = describe(img_b, asift_detect(img_b))\nmatches = matcher(fa, fb, size(img_a), size(img_b))\n\nThe package's examples/loma.jl runs this on a pair of downloaded images and draws the matches. Cite the LoMa and DeDoDe papers when you use these networks (see the README).","title":"LoMa"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Developer-Notes","page":"Developer Notes","text":"This page is for people who work on the package itself. Users need none of it.","title":"Developer Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#The-repository","page":"Developer Notes","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures lives in the VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures/ directory of the VisualGeometryToolkit.jl repository, a Julia workspace of several packages with one Manifest at its root. Instantiate it once per clone, from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nThe repository ships git hooks in .githooks/ that re-resolve the root Manifest after a pull, checkout or rebase that changes a Project.toml. They are opt-in, once per clone:\n\ngit config core.hooksPath .githooks","title":"The repository"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Tests","page":"Developer Notes","text":"The suite uses ReTestItems; the items live in test/*_tests.jl. From the repository root:\n\njulia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\njulia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test(; test_args = [\"polarity\"])'\n\nThe second form runs the items whose names match the pattern. Check the total it reports: a pattern that matches nothing runs nothing.\n\ntest/readme_tests.jl runs every julia block of the README except the installation block, and checks each statement whose line ends in # true. Keep the README's quick start self-contained: no files, no network.","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Documentation","page":"Developer Notes","text":"The docs index is generated from the README by docs/make.jl, so edit the README rather than docs/src/index.md. Build from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures/docs --startup-file=no VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures/docs/make.jl\n\nThe build runs the doctests and fails on an exported name without a docstring.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Benchmarks-and-examples","page":"Developer Notes","text":"benchmark/mc holds the Monte Carlo studies of the detector, which are not part of the test suite: keypoint_uncertainty_mc.jl calibrates the keypoint covariance, and zeisl_comparison.jl compares it with Zeisl et al. (BMVC 2009). Both write their results to benchmark/mc/results/, which is not tracked.\n\nexamples/ has its own environment, outside the workspace, because its CUDA and cuDNN pins would otherwise hold back the CUDA of every package's tests. Instantiate it with julia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures/examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'.","title":"Benchmarks and examples"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Accuracy-bounds-that-other-code-relies-on","page":"Developer Notes","text":"The refinement accuracy pinned by the test suite (two detections of the same small, isolated blob differ by at most 0.06 px) is what VisualGeometryToolkitCore's coincidence gate is built around. That gate, COINCIDENT_PX = 0.1 px, supplied as tol_px in Core's src/geometry/model_validity.jl, rejects minimal samples with coincident points, and it is set to clear the 0.06 px bound with margin. A change to the refinement that loosens the bound therefore changes which samples Core's estimators reject. Coordinate it with Core instead of retuning the test here.","title":"Accuracy bounds that other code relies on"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Serialization","page":"Developer Notes","text":"The package writes no file format. Detections, feature frames, Size2 and quantities are serialized by VisualGeometryToolkitIO (json_value, from_json), which owns the stack's quantity JSON format: numbers in the record's declared units. The {\"value\", \"unit\"} objects this package's JSON.jl hooks once wrote are not read; a file holding them is migrated once (vgio_migrate).","title":"Serialization"},{"category":"section","location":"api/matching/#Matching","page":"Matching","text":"Strategies, metrics and matches; the matchers on the CPU, on a GPU and over stacked galleries; described features; the LoMa networks.","title":"Matching"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.nn_match","page":"Matching","text":"nn_match(desc1, desc2, strategy = MutualNN(); device = nothing, kw...) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nmatch on device, any KernelAbstractions backend (device = nothing is KA.CPU()). Every backend runs the same GEMM and selection kernels, so a GPU (e.g. CUDABackend()) returns what the CPU returns, with every keyword match accepts: metric, dm, ratio_threshold, min_similarity, max_distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.nn_match"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CspondSet","page":"Matching","text":"CspondSet(features::AbstractVector)\nCspondSet(features::Tuple)\nCspondSet(T::Type)\n\nAn N-way correspondence: the same scene point observed as one feature in each of several images, where Cspond is the two-way case. It wraps a vector of features and iterates, indexes and has a length like one. A tuple becomes a fixed-size SVector; a Vector (or CspondSet(T), an empty one of element type T) grows with push! and append!.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> track = CspondSet(Int);\n\njulia> push!(track, 3); push!(track, 7);\n\njulia> length(track), track[2], collect(track)\n(2, 7, [3, 7])\n\njulia> length(CspondSet((1, 2, 3)))\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CspondSet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CosineSimilarity","page":"Matching","text":"CosineSimilarity <: DescriptorSimilarity\n\nCosine similarity for L2-NORMALIZED descriptors, computed as the dot product a·b — no normalization is applied, so un-normalized input gives raw dot products. Range [-1, 1] for unit vectors, 1 = identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.CosineSimilarity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorDistance","page":"Matching","text":"DescriptorDistance <: DescriptorMetric\n\nDistance metrics: lower values = more similar. NN finds minima.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorDistance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorMetric","page":"Matching","text":"DescriptorMetric\n\nAbstract type for descriptor comparison metrics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorMetric"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorSimilarity","page":"Matching","text":"DescriptorSimilarity <: DescriptorMetric\n\nSimilarity metrics: higher values = more similar. NN finds maxima.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescriptorSimilarity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.KnnMatch","page":"Matching","text":"KnnMatch(k) <: NNStrategy\n\nk-nearest-neighbor matching: for each desc1 row, keep its k nearest desc2 columns instead of only the single best. Permissive by design — surfaces true matches that sit at rank 2..k or just under a strict cutoff (common with repetitive patterns, where a confuser can out-rank the true neighbor). Downstream geometry (e.g. RANSAC) disambiguates the extra candidates. Pair with min_similarity to bound the kept scores.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.KnnMatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.L2","page":"Matching","text":"L2 <: DescriptorDistance\n\nEuclidean distance: ‖a - b‖. Range [0, 2] for unit vectors, 0 = identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.L2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MutualNN","page":"Matching","text":"MutualNN <: NNStrategy\n\nMutual nearest-neighbor matching: find NN in both directions, keep only pairs where both directions agree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MutualNN"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NN","page":"Matching","text":"NN <: NNStrategy\n\nOne-way nearest-neighbor matching: for each descriptor in desc1, find the nearest neighbor in desc2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NN"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNMatch","page":"Matching","text":"NNMatch\n\nA nearest-neighbor match: Attributed{Cspond{Int,Int}, NNMatchMeta}.\n\nProperty forwarding gives direct access:\n\nm.first — index into desc1\nm.second — index into desc2\nm.score — match quality (similarity or distance, depending on metric)\nm.ratio — Lowe's SNN ratio\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNMatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNMatchMeta","page":"Matching","text":"NNMatchMeta\n\nMetadata for a nearest-neighbor match.\n\nFields\n\nscore::Float32: Match quality in the metric's native units (similarity for CosineSimilarity, distance for L2)\nratio::Float32: Lowe's ratio — d_best / d_2nd for a distance metric, s_2nd / s_best for a similarity, so that smaller = more distinctive whatever the metric. Inf32 when there is no second neighbour, and when a similarity's best score is non-positive: the ratio is undefined there, and no ratio test admits it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNMatchMeta"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNStrategy","page":"Matching","text":"NNStrategy\n\nAbstract type for nearest-neighbor matching strategies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NNStrategy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.csponds-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Attributed{Pair{Int64, Int64}, NNMatchMeta}}}","page":"Matching","text":"csponds(features1, features2, matches::AbstractVector{NNMatch})\n\nConvert index-based NN matches to geometric correspondences.\n\nReturns a columnar Csponds whose endpoints are the matched features and whose attribute columns carry BOTH match statistics:\n\nscore::Vector{Float64} — higher-is-better sampling prior (similarity metrics are used as is; distance metrics are inverted 1/(d + 1e-6))\nratio::Vector{Float32} — the Lowe's-ratio value of each match (previously computed and then dropped; it now survives to downstream consumers)\n\nAccess endpoints via sources/targets, attributes via cs.score / cs.ratio; subsetting keeps all columns aligned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.csponds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match","page":"Matching","text":"match(desc1, desc2, [strategy]; metric, dm, ratio_threshold,\n      min_similarity, max_distance) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nMatch descriptors using nearest-neighbor search, on the CPU. It runs the same KernelAbstractions matcher as nn_match on a GPU, so a device returns what this returns. Descriptors are compared in their own float type.\n\nPublic but not exported, because it would shadow Base.match: import it (using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures: match) or call VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match.\n\nArguments\n\ndesc1::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: N1×D descriptor matrix (rows = descriptors)\ndesc2::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: N2×D descriptor matrix\nstrategy::NNStrategy: MutualNN() (default), NN() or KnnMatch(k)\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nmetric::DescriptorMetric: CosineSimilarity() (default) or L2()\ndm: Precomputed N1×N2 pairwise matrix (skip pairwise_matrix computation)\nratio_threshold: Lowe's SNN ratio threshold; nothing = disabled\nmin_similarity: Minimum score for DescriptorSimilarity metrics\nmax_distance: Maximum score for DescriptorDistance metrics\n\nReturns\n\nVector{NNMatch} sorted by score (best first).\n\nUsage\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures: match, pairwise_matrix, MutualNN, NN, CosineSimilarity, L2\n\n# Cosine similarity (default) — good for L2-normalized descriptors\nmatch(d1, d2, MutualNN())\nmatch(d1, d2, MutualNN(); min_similarity=0.7)\n\n# L2 distance\nmatch(d1, d2, MutualNN(); metric=L2(), max_distance=0.8)\n\n# Precomputed distance matrix\ndm = pairwise_matrix(CosineSimilarity(), d1, d2)\nmatch(d1, d2, MutualNN(); dm)\n\n# With Lowe's ratio test\nmatch(d1, d2, NN(); ratio_threshold=0.8)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_one_to_one!-Tuple{AbstractVector{Bool}, AbstractVector{Bool}, Any}","page":"Matching","text":"resolve_one_to_one!(used_a::AbstractVector{Bool}, used_b::AbstractVector{Bool},\n                    candidates; a = c -> c.first, b = c -> c.second) -> Vector{Int}\n\nGreedy one-to-one reduction over an ALREADY-ORDERED candidate list: visit candidates in the given order, keep a candidate when neither of its endpoints is used yet, and mark both endpoints used. Returns the kept indices into candidates (in visit order); used_a/used_b are mutated in place, so pre-seeded entries (e.g. matches certified by an earlier stage) are respected and callers can chain several candidate lists through one pair of masks.\n\na/b extract the two endpoint indices from a candidate — the defaults serve NNMatch (m.first/m.second); supply accessors for tuple lists.\n\nThe caller owns the ORDER: sort best-first (descending similarity, ascending distance) for best-first assignment (the reduction MutualNN sets already satisfy, and the one KnnMatch(k) sets need), by overlap for geometric association, etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_one_to_one!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_one_to_one-Tuple{AbstractVector{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Attributed{Pair{Int64, Int64}, NNMatchMeta}}, Int64, Int64}","page":"Matching","text":"resolve_one_to_one(matches::AbstractVector{NNMatch}, n_a, n_b;\n                   metric = CosineSimilarity()) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nBest-first one-to-one reduction of an NNMatch list: visit the best match first and keep each first/second index at most once. metric must be the metric the matches were scored with, because it decides what \"best\" means: the highest score for a DescriptorSimilarity (the default, CosineSimilarity()), the lowest for a DescriptorDistance such as L2(). n_a/n_b bound the two index spaces. MutualNN output is returned unchanged (already one-to-one); KnnMatch(k) output collapses to its best consistent assignment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.resolve_one_to_one"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._nn_select-Tuple{NNStrategy, DescriptorMetric, KernelAbstractions.Backend, Vector{Int64}, Vector{Int64}}","page":"Matching","text":"_nn_select(strategy, metric, dev, offsets, sizes; gal, feat, S, chunk_bytes)\n    -> (top_idx, top_sc, rev_idx)\n\nThe selection behind every match, on backend dev. The gallery is the rows, cut into blocks (offsets, 0-based, and sizes); the scores are either computed from descriptors already on dev (gal, rows × D, and feat, features × D, in the score type) a row range at a time, or read from a precomputed matrix S (rows × features). Returns host arrays: per row the top k feature indices and their signed scores (rows × k), and for MutualNN each feature's best row within each block (features × blocks, block-local), else nothing.\n\nThe score block is the pipeline's largest single allocation (M × N: 3.09 GiB for a 60k gallery against 13.7k detections, 9.79 GiB against 43.5k). Materialising it whole is what OOMs on cluttered frames, so it is built _row_chunks at a time and each block released eagerly via KA.unsafe_free! rather than waiting for a finalizer. The result does not depend on the chunking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._nn_select"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._row_chunks-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Int64, Int64, Type{T}, Integer}} where T","page":"Matching","text":"_row_chunks(backend, M, N, T, chunk_bytes) -> Vector{Tuple{Int, Int}}\n\nConsecutive ROW ranges of an M-row gallery whose score block (rows × N of T) fits the budget. chunk_bytes > 0 is honoured verbatim; otherwise the budget is _SCORE_BUDGET of device_free_bytes, which returns typemax on backends exposing no query — yielding a single chunk, the historical behaviour.\n\nRows, not whole galleries: one calibration target with thousands of blobs is ONE gallery whose block alone can exceed the card (9.3 GiB against a 40k-feature frame, 2026-09-15), so a gallery may span several chunks. That is still exact — the forward sweep is per row, and the reverse sweep merges a gallery's per-column best across its chunks (_ka_top1_cols_blocked!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._row_chunks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.pairwise_matrix","page":"Matching","text":"pairwise_matrix(metric, desc1, desc2, device = KA.CPU()) -> Matrix\n\nThe N1×N2 pairwise comparison matrix: a·b for CosineSimilarity(), ‖a − b‖ for L2(), in the descriptors' float type (Float32 descriptors give Float32, Float64 give Float64). The GEMM runs on device, any KernelAbstractions backend (CUDABackend(), …); the matrix is returned on the host. One implementation serves every backend: there is no separate host method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.pairwise_matrix"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.StackedDescriptorIndex","page":"Matching","text":"StackedDescriptorIndex(backend, galleries::AbstractDict)\n\nDevice-resident stack of descriptor galleries: all galleries' descriptor rows in one matrix, plus per-gallery offsets/sizes for the block-local kernels. galleries maps keys to descriptor matrices (rows = features) or to anything descriptor_matrix accepts. The stack keeps the galleries' float type (Float32 galleries stack as Float32, Float64 as Float64). Build once, reuse across nn_match_stacked calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.StackedDescriptorIndex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.nn_match_stacked-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{NNStrategy, AbstractDict{K}, Any}} where K","page":"Matching","text":"nn_match_stacked(strategy, galleries::AbstractDict, features;\n                 backend, index = nothing, min_similarity = 0.6, chunk_bytes = 0)\n    -> Dict{K, Vector{NNMatch}}\n\nMatch features against every gallery in galleries with one stacked GEMM. Per gallery the result is identical to matching that gallery alone with strategy (MutualNN reverse checks are block-local; KnnMatch(k) takes any k): it is the matcher match runs, with the galleries as blocks of its rows. Matches use LOCAL indices: m.first indexes into gallery k's rows, m.second into features. Supply a prebuilt StackedDescriptorIndex as index to skip the per-call stack/upload.\n\nWhen index is given it — not galleries — defines what gets matched: the returned dictionary has one entry per index.keys, and galleries supplies only the key type K. The two key sets therefore need NOT agree, and callers rely on that: a caller narrowing its gallery set across calls can keep ONE index built over the full set rather than restaging descriptors every call, and simply ignore the extra entries. Build the index from the widest set you will ever match against.\n\nThe score matrix is built a row range at a time. chunk_bytes > 0 caps each range's score block (rows × features) at that many bytes; the default 0 sizes the ranges from free device memory. The result does not depend on the chunking. Scores are cosine similarities in the wider of the index's and the features' float type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.nn_match_stacked"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescribedFeature","page":"Matching","text":"DescribedFeature{D<:Detection, N}\n\nA feature detection paired with a fixed-length descriptor vector.\n\nWraps a Detection (frame + metadata) with an SVector{N, Float32} descriptor. All detection properties (.center, .σ, .frame, .metadata, etc.) are forwarded transparently via @generated getproperty.\n\nUse with StructArray for efficient columnar storage and zero-copy descriptor matrix extraction:\n\nsa = described_features(detections, desc_matrix)\nmatches = VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match(sa_board, sa_query, MutualNN())\nscored = csponds(sa_board, sa_query, matches)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.DescribedFeature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.described_features-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection}, AbstractMatrix{Float32}}","page":"Matching","text":"described_features(detections, desc_matrix) -> StructArray{DescribedFeature}\n\nBuild a StructArray of DescribedFeature from a detection vector and an N×D descriptor matrix. The descriptor dimension D becomes the static size parameter of each SVector{D, Float32}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.described_features"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.descriptor_matrix-Tuple{StructArrays.StructArray{<:DescribedFeature}}","page":"Matching","text":"descriptor_matrix(sa::StructArray{<:DescribedFeature}) -> AbstractMatrix{Float32}\n\nZero-copy N×D descriptor matrix view from a StructArray of DescribedFeature. Suitable for supplying directly to match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.descriptor_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match-2","page":"Matching","text":"match(f1::StructArray{<:DescribedFeature}, f2::StructArray{<:DescribedFeature},\n      strategy; kwargs...) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nMatch described features by their descriptors. Extracts descriptor matrices and delegates to the matrix-based match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.match"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaDescriptor","page":"Matching","text":"LoMaDescriptor(variant = :b; max_detections = 2048, bf16 = false, execution_provider = :cpu)\n\nA DeDoDe descriptor of LoMa: :b (DeDoDe-B, 128-dimensional, 54 MB) or :g (DeDoDe-G, 256-dimensional, 1.3 GB; 690 MB with bf16 = true, which needs execution_provider = :cuda). The model is downloaded on first use and cached. (descriptor)(img, detections) describes the max_detections strongest detections (largest |response|) of img and returns a StructArray{DescribedFeature}. execution_provider = :cuda needs import CUDA, cuDNN and a functional device.\n\nThe network is DeDoDe's (Edstedt et al., 3DV 2024), as exported by LoMa; cite both (BibTeX in the package README).\n\nimg is a (3, 784, 784) Float32 RGB channelview in [0, 1], as Float32.(channelview(rgb)) gives it: the exported graph's input shape is fixed, and it feeds the first convolution directly, with no normalization. Do not ImageNet-normalize it: on LoMa's toronto pair that drops the matches from 166 to 14 (and none above probability 0.5).\n\nA descriptor pairs with the LoMaMatchers of its dimension: :b with :b128, :g with :b, :l, :g and :r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaDescriptor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaMatcher","page":"Matching","text":"LoMaMatcher(variant = :b128; bf16 = false, execution_provider = :cpu)\n\nA LoMa matcher: :b128 (48 MB) on 128-dimensional DeDoDe-B descriptors, or :b (48 MB), :l (184 MB), :g (726 MB) and :r (48 MB) on 256-dimensional DeDoDe-G ones (LoMaDescriptor(:g)). bf16 = true takes the half-size bf16 export of any of them, which needs execution_provider = :cuda (ONNX Runtime's CPU provider lacks bf16 kernels). The model is downloaded on first use and cached; see the call method for matching. execution_provider = :cuda needs import CUDA, cuDNN and a functional device.\n\nThe network is LoMa's (Nordström et al., \"LoMa: Local Feature Matching Revisited\", ECCV 2026); cite it (BibTeX in the package README).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaMatcher"},{"category":"method","location":"api/matching/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaMatcher-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Matching","text":"(matcher::LoMaMatcher)(features_a, features_b, size_a, size_b; min_score = 0) -> Vector{NNMatch}\n\nMatch two sets of LoMaDescriptor features, whose descriptor dimension must be the matcher's (ArgumentError otherwise). size_a/size_b are the image sizes, (H, W) or a channelview's (C, H, W).\n\nReturns the same type as match: m.first indexes features_a, m.second indexes features_b, sorted best-first. m.score is the network's match probability (higher is better, a similarity, so the default csponds(features_a, features_b, matches) reads it correctly); m.ratio is Inf32, since the network has no second neighbour. Matches scoring below min_score are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.LoMaMatcher"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Detection","page":"Detection","text":"","title":"Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#The-ASIFT-detector","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detect is the package's detector. It implements IMAS-25, the affine-invariant SIFT of the IMAS family. The image is resampled under 25 simulated camera tilts (the identity, 8 tilts of 2.62 and 16 tilts of 5.18 at evenly spaced longitudes). DoG extrema are found in each warp, refined to sub-pixel accuracy, mapped back to the input image as elliptic frames, and merged across warps by a non-maximum suppression. The whole pipeline runs as KernelAbstractions kernels, so the same code runs on the CPU (the default) and on any GPU backend (device = CUDABackend(), for instance).\n\nThe tilt schedule is a keyword. tilts = [(1.0, 0.0)], the identity alone, is plain DoG-SIFT:\n\ndets = asift_detect(img)                          # IMAS-25\nsift = asift_detect(img; tilts = [(1.0, 0.0)])    # DoG-SIFT\n\nThe other keywords choose the non-maximum suppression (nms = :mxm, :ellipse or :none), gate by polarity (polarity = :dark or :light), restrict suppression to features of similar scale (band_octaves), and set the detection thresholds. The asift_detect docstring lists them.","title":"The ASIFT detector"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Plans-and-their-cache","page":"Detection","text":"The detector's buffers for one image size, device and tilt schedule are an AsiftPlan. asift_detect builds plans on demand and keeps the most recent one: a plan for a large image under the full schedule holds several GiB of device memory. Process frames grouped by resolution to reuse it. set_max_cached_plans! keeps more plans, and clear_asift_plans! releases them. To manage a plan yourself, build it and call asift_detect!, which returns the raw ImasFeatureKA records; asift_detections converts them.","title":"Plans and their cache"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Pixel-origin","page":"Detection","text":"Every detector of the package returns COLMAP pixel centres. The top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5), so the pixel img[r, c] has its centre at (c − 0.5, r − 0.5). A center is (x, y): x along the image columns and y along the rows.\n\nasift_detect(img; raw = true) returns the raw features instead, in 0-indexed OpenCV coordinates (top-left pixel centre at (0, 0)), which are 0.5 less on both axes.\n\nThe package follows the stack's rule that a function returns results in the frame its inputs came in: pixels in, pixels out. The matchers work in descriptor space and never touch coordinates, so nothing here normalizes.","title":"Pixel origin"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Feature-frames-are-shapes","page":"Detection","text":"A Detection composes a feature frame with the detector's metadata. \"Frame\" is meant in the VLFeat and Kornia sense: the shape of a detected feature, not a coordinate system. The frames are VisualGeometryToolkitCore types, ordered by degrees of freedom:\n\nFrame DOF Shape\nDisc 3 centre and isotropic scale\nOrientedDisc 4 centre, scale and orientation\nEllipticFrame 5 centre and moment matrix (anisotropic)\nAffineFrame 6 centre and full affine shape\n\nasift_detect returns Detection{EllipticFrame}. The accessor is feature_frame(d). Properties of the frame and of the metadata read directly: d.center, d.σ (the characteristic scale), d.Σ (the moment matrix), d.response.\n\nCoordinate-frame tags, such as ImageFrame and NormalizedImageFrame, are a separate vocabulary that lives in VisualGeometryToolkitCore. The two share the word \"frame\" and nothing else.","title":"Feature frames are shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Polarity","page":"Detection","text":"ASIFT records the sign of the DoG response in the metadata field laplacian_scale_score: positive for a dark blob on a lighter background and negative for a light one. is_dark and is_light read it. Detections whose metadata has no such field are neither; the trait polarity_trait tells the two kinds of metadata apart.","title":"Polarity"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Accuracy-of-the-sub-pixel-refinement","page":"Detection","text":"The quadratic sub-pixel refinement pulls a centre toward the nearest pixel centre. On noise-free isotropic Gaussian blobs on the CPU, measured by the test suite:\n\na blob centred on a pixel centre comes back exact, within 0.005 px;\nbetween pixel centres the pull is at most 0.03 px at σ = 4, and at most 0.06 px for σ from 2 to 8.\n\nLarger blobs and clutter do worse. The Monte Carlo study in the package's benchmark/mc directory measured a centre bias of 0.1 to 0.2 px on clean blobs with σ ≥ 6.5, and up to about 1 px in clutter. Treat 0.06 px as the bound for small, isolated blobs only.","title":"Accuracy of the sub-pixel refinement"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/detection/#Keypoint-uncertainty","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detect(img; uncertainty = FeatureCovariance()) returns each detection with its frame as VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Uncertain(frame, Σ̃). cov(d) is a 3×3 covariance over (x, y, log σ) in the image frame, for unit iid pixel noise in the detector's intensity units (the [0, 1] scale for 8-bit input). The physical covariance is σ_n²·Σ̃ for a noise standard deviation σ_n. Consumers that need only the position take the top-left 2×2 block.\n\nThe covariance follows from the implicit function theorem at the refined DoG extremum; its noise term is tabulated exactly for the discrete pyramid once per plan. The FeatureCovariance docstring states the model. The Monte Carlo study that calibrates it is benchmark/mc/keypoint_uncertainty_mc.jl, and benchmark/mc/zeisl_comparison.jl compares it with the covariance of Zeisl et al. (BMVC 2009). The default, uncertainty = nothing, compiles the detector exactly as without the option.","title":"Keypoint uncertainty"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"This page installs the package and walks through the README's quick start one step at a time. Every block on this page is a doctest, run by the docs build.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"Install VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures from the General registry (Julia 1.12 or later):\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\")\n\nThe geometric types come from VisualGeometryToolkitCore, which is installed as a dependency. Add it to your environment as well (Pkg.add(\"VisualGeometryToolkitCore\")) when you construct frames or points yourself.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#A-synthetic-image","page":"Getting Started","text":"The detector takes an H×W matrix: Gray pixels, UInt8, or real values in [0, 1]. The image below is a gray field with three Gaussian blobs, each given as (row, column, σ, amplitude). A positive amplitude makes a light blob and a negative one a dark blob.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> blobs = [(40, 50, 4.0, 0.35), (70, 100, 3.0, -0.3), (30, 130, 6.0, 0.3)];\n\njulia> img = [0.5 + sum(a * exp(-((r - y)^2 + (c - x)^2) / (2s^2)) for (y, x, s, a) in blobs)\n              for r in 1:120, c in 1:160];\n\njulia> size(img)\n(120, 160)","title":"A synthetic image"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Detecting-features","page":"Getting Started","text":"asift_detect runs the IMAS-25 ASIFT detector: the image is warped by 25 simulated camera tilts, DoG extrema are found in each warp, and the detections are mapped back to the input image and merged by a cross-warp non-maximum suppression. Each result is a Detection: an elliptic feature frame with the detector's metadata.\n\njulia> dets = asift_detect(img);\n\njulia> length(dets)\n3\n\njulia> dark = only(filter(is_dark, dets));\n\njulia> round.(dark.center; digits = 1)    # (x, y): column, then row\n2-element GeometryBasics.Point{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n 99.5\n 69.5\n\njulia> round(dark.σ; digits = 1)\n3.0\n\njulia> count(is_light, dets)\n2\n\nThe centre is in COLMAP pixel coordinates, where the top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5). The blob placed at row 70 and column 100 is therefore found at (99.5, 69.5). Detection explains the coordinates, the accuracy of the sub-pixel refinement and the other options of the detector.\n\nThe metadata reads as properties of the detection:\n\njulia> propertynames(dark.metadata)\n(:response, :edge_score, :laplacian_scale_score, :warp_idx)\n\njulia> dark.response > 0    # a dark blob has a positive DoG response\ntrue","title":"Detecting features"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Matching-descriptors","page":"Getting Started","text":"nn_match matches two descriptor matrices, one descriptor per row. By default it keeps mutual nearest neighbours under the cosine similarity, so the descriptors should have unit length. Here the second set is the first, shuffled and perturbed:\n\njulia> using Random\n\njulia> rng = Xoshiro(1);\n\njulia> unit_rows(D) = D ./ sqrt.(sum(abs2, D; dims = 2));\n\njulia> D1 = unit_rows(randn(rng, Float32, 100, 32));\n\njulia> perm = randperm(rng, 100);\n\njulia> D2 = unit_rows(D1[perm, :] .+ 0.05f0 .* randn(rng, Float32, 100, 32));\n\njulia> matches = nn_match(D1, D2);\n\njulia> length(matches)\n100\n\njulia> all(perm[m.second] == m.first for m in matches)\ntrue\n\nEach match is an NNMatch: m.first indexes the rows of D1, m.second those of D2, and m.score and m.ratio are the similarity and Lowe's ratio. Matching covers the other strategies and metrics, the GPU and the learned LoMa matchers.","title":"Matching descriptors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Saving-detections","page":"Getting Started","text":"Detections are written to and read from JSON by VisualGeometryToolkitIO (json_value(d, contract; kind) and from_json(Detection, obj, contract; kind)), which owns the stack's quantity JSON format; its Serialization guide describes the format.","title":"Saving detections"},{"category":"section","location":"api/detection/#Detection","page":"Detection","text":"The detection type and its polarity helpers, the ASIFT detector and its plans, the keypoint covariance, the image-input helpers, and the linear-radiance PNG reader (load_linear_image).","title":"Detection"},{"category":"module","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures","page":"Detection","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\nFeature detection and descriptor matching.\n\nDetection: ASIFT (IMAS-25) on KernelAbstractions. asift_detect runs on the CPU or any KernelAbstractions GPU backend and returns Detections (a frame plus metadata). VisualGeometryToolkitIO writes and reads them as JSON (json_value, from_json).\nMatching: nearest-neighbour descriptor matching, one KernelAbstractions implementation on the CPU or any GPU backend (nn_match, match), and a stacked matcher over many galleries (nn_match_stacked).\nLoMa: the learned DeDoDe descriptors and LoMa matchers, from the authors' ONNX exports (LoMaDescriptor, LoMaMatcher).\nExtensions: JLD2 backs FileCache; Makie draws detections and correspondences.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc}}","page":"Detection","text":"Disc(d::Detection) -> Disc\n\nExtract or convert to a Disc frame (uses characteristic_scale for anisotropic).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Disc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.FeatureFrame}}","page":"Detection","text":"Ellipse(d::Detection; cutoff=1.0) -> Ellipse\n\nConvert to an ellipse at the cutoff·σ contour (via region).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.Ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame}}","page":"Detection","text":"EllipticFrame(d::Detection) -> EllipticFrame\n\nExtract or convert to an EllipticFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Primitives.EllipticFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.Detection","page":"Detection","text":"Detection{F<:FeatureFrame, M}\n\nType alias for Attributed{F, M} — a detection result composing a geometric frame with detector-specific metadata.\n\nThe frame type F determines the geometry:\n\nDetection{Disc{T}, M} - isotropic detection (3 DOF)\nDetection{OrientedDisc{T}, M} - oriented isotropic detection (4 DOF)\nDetection{EllipticFrame{T}, M} - anisotropic detection (5 DOF)\nDetection{AffineFrame{T}, M} - full affine detection (6 DOF)\nDetection{<:Uncertain{F}, M} - a frame F with its covariance (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Uncertain(frame, Σ)), from asift_detect(…; uncertainty = FeatureCovariance()): cov(d) is the covariance, feature_frame(d) the bare frame, geom(d) the detection without it, and the properties below read as on the bare detection\n\nType Parameters\n\nF<:FeatureFrame: The geometric frame type\nM: Metadata, a NamedTuple. ASIFT emits (response, edge_score, laplacian_scale_score, warp_idx); laplacian_scale_score is the polarity field is_dark/is_light read (> 0 dark, < 0 light).\n\nFields (from Attributed)\n\nvalue::F: The geometric frame (center + shape)\nattributes::M: Detector-specific scores and information\n\nCoordinates\n\nThe frame's center is (x, y) in COLMAP pixel-centre coordinates, the convention of every detector in this package: x along the image columns, y along the rows, the top-left pixel's centre at (0.5, 0.5). The pixel img[r, c] has its centre at (c − 0.5, r − 0.5). asift_detect documents its measured sub-pixel accuracy.\n\nProperty Access\n\nProperties are forwarded from frame and metadata:\n\nd.frame, d.value → the geometric frame\nd.metadata, d.attributes → the metadata\nd.center → frame.center\nd.σ → scale from frame (exact for Disc, characteristic_scale for anisotropic)\nd.Σ → moment matrix (blob shape, not measurement covariance)\nd.response, d.edge_score, etc. → forwarded from metadata\n\nExample\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> det = Detection(Disc(Point2(100.0, 200.0), 5.0), (response = 0.05, laplacian_scale_score = -0.1));\n\njulia> Tuple(det.center), det.σ, det.response   # from the frame and the metadata\n((100.0, 200.0), 5.0, 0.05)\n\njulia> is_light(det)\ntrue\n\nSee also: Disc, EllipticFrame, VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Attributed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.Detection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarity","page":"Detection","text":"HasPolarity()\n\nThe HasPolarityTrait of detection metadata that records a polarity: a NamedTuple with a laplacian_scale_score field, positive for a dark blob and negative for a light one. is_dark and is_light read that field's sign. Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarityTrait","page":"Detection","text":"HasPolarityTrait\n\nHoly trait for metadata types indicating whether they contain polarity information: HasPolarity or NoPolarity, as polarity_trait returns it. Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarityTrait"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NoPolarity","page":"Detection","text":"NoPolarity()\n\nThe HasPolarityTrait of detection metadata that records no polarity. is_dark and is_light are both false for such a detection. Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NoPolarity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.is_dark-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection}","page":"Detection","text":"is_dark(d::Detection) -> Bool\n\nWhether d is a dark blob on a lighter background: its metadata records a polarity (see polarity_trait) and its laplacian_scale_score is positive. false for a detection without a recorded polarity.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> is_dark(Detection(Disc(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0), 0.5; polarity = :dark))\ntrue\n\njulia> is_dark(Detection(Disc(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0), 0.5))   # no polarity recorded\nfalse\n\nSee also is_light.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.is_dark"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.is_light-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractDetection}","page":"Detection","text":"is_light(d::Detection) -> Bool\n\nWhether d is a light blob on a darker background: its metadata records a polarity (see polarity_trait) and its laplacian_scale_score is negative. false for a detection without a recorded polarity. ASIFT detections record one, from the sign of the DoG response.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> d = Detection(Disc(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0), 0.5; polarity = :light);\n\njulia> is_light(d), is_dark(d)\n(true, false)\n\nSee also is_dark.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.is_light"},{"category":"function","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.polarity_trait","page":"Detection","text":"polarity_trait(::Type{M}) -> HasPolarityTrait\npolarity_trait(d::Detection) -> HasPolarityTrait\n\nWhether metadata of type M, or the metadata of the detection d, records a polarity: HasPolarity for a NamedTuple with a laplacian_scale_score field, NoPolarity otherwise. A detector whose metadata is another type opts in by adding a method for its type, and must then give it a laplacian_scale_score property. Public, not exported.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> import VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures as VgtkFeatures\n\njulia> VgtkFeatures.polarity_trait(Detection(Disc(Point2(1.0, 2.0), 3.0), (response = 1.0,)))\nVisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.NoPolarity()\n\njulia> VgtkFeatures.polarity_trait(typeof((response = 1.0, laplacian_scale_score = -1.0)))\nVisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.HasPolarity()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.polarity_trait"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gray_f32-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:ColorTypes.Gray{<:AbstractFloat}}}","page":"Detection","text":"gray_f32(img) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nimg as float gray: a float Gray matrix or a bare Float32 matrix (a device array already in the kernels' form) is returned UNTOUCHED — no copy, no wrap, so a CuArray{Float32} stays on the device; anything else (fixed-point gray, any colorant) converts to Gray{Float32}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gray_f32"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gray_u8-Tuple{Matrix{UInt8}}","page":"Detection","text":"gray_u8(img) -> Matrix{UInt8}\n\nimg as H×W gray bytes, in a Matrix{UInt8} the caller owns. A Matrix{UInt8} is returned as is; a Gray{N0f8} matrix is copied byte for byte (its pixels already are bytes); everything else is quantised through Gray{N0f8} (float colorants LOSE precision here by design — use gray_f32 where sub-pixel photometry matters).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gray_u8"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MserRegion","page":"Detection","text":"MserRegion\n\nOne maximally stable extremal region, as detect_mser returns it.\n\nFields\n\nseed::CartesianIndex{2}: the region's reference pixel, a pixel at the region's level (the tree node MaxTree chose; VLFeat may pick another pixel of the same plateau). A VLFeat seed s (0-based linear index into the column-major H × W image given with dims = (H, W)) is the pixel CartesianIndices(img)[s + 1], and ours is VLFeat's index LinearIndices(img)[seed] - 1.\nlevel::UInt8: the region's level in the input image: a :dark region is the 8-connected component of img .<= level holding seed, a :light one that of img .>= level. img[seed] == level.\narea::Int: its number of pixels.\nvariation::Float32: VLFeat's (in)stability (|R₊Δ| − |R|) / |R|.\npolarity::Symbol: :dark or :light.\n\nThe seed and level determine the region, so its pixels and boundary are not stored (nested regions would hold every pixel many times over): they are formed on demand from the image the region was detected in — region_mask(region, img) the pixels as a mask (VLFeat's vl_erfill), boundary_contours(region, img) their boundary as closed polygons. is_dark and is_light read the polarity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MserRegion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.boundary_contours-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}}","page":"Detection","text":"boundary_contours(pixels) -> Vector{Vector{Point2{Float64}}}\nboundary_contours(region::MserRegion, img) -> Vector{Vector{Point2{Float64}}}\n\nThe boundary of a set of pixels (CartesianIndex{2} (row, column), a Bool mask, or an MSER of img — its region_mask), traced along the pixel edges: one closed polygon per boundary curve (the last vertex joins the first), vertices only where the boundary turns, with x along the columns and y along the rows in the COLMAP pixel origin (conventions/pixel-origins.md), so the pixel (r, c) is the square [c − 1, c] × [r − 1, r] and every vertex is a pixel corner with integer coordinates. The polygons are sorted by signed shoelace area, descending: the outer boundaries first (positive, clockwise on screen), the largest first, then the holes (negative), the smallest first and the largest last. Diagonal neighbours are connected (8-connectivity, the connectivity of an MSER), so where two pixels of the set meet at a corner one curve runs through it; holes are 4-connected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.boundary_contours"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detect_mser-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Detection","text":"detect_mser(img; delta = 5, min_area = 3 / length(img), max_area = 0.75,\n            max_variation = 0.25, min_diversity = 0.2,\n            bright_on_dark = true, dark_on_bright = true) -> Vector{MserRegion}\n\nThe maximally stable extremal regions (MSER; Matas, Chum, Urban and Pajdla, \"Robust wide baseline stereo from maximally stable extremal regions\", BMVC\n\nof the 8-bit gray image img, selected as VLFeat's vl_mser selects\n\nthem (Andrea Vedaldi and Brian Fulkerson, BSD license; src/mser.jl carries the notice). VLFeat's stability criterion is its own, simpler than the paper's.\n\nThe extremal regions are the 8-connected components of the level sets img .<= l, arranged in a tree by ImageMorphology's MaxTree. The variation of a region R at level l is (|R₊Δ| − |R|) / |R|, where R₊Δ is the largest region containing R at a level at most l + delta. Of a region and its parent region exactly one level up, the one of larger variation (on a tie, the child) is not maximally stable; the root never is. The survivors are then filtered, largest first:\n\nmax_variation: a region of variation >= max_variation is dropped;\nmax_area, min_area: FRACTIONS of length(img); a region larger than max_area * length(img) or smaller than min_area * length(img) pixels is dropped (the default min_area is three pixels);\nmin_diversity: a region whose area differs from that of its nearest surviving enclosing region by less than min_diversity times the latter's area is a duplicate and is dropped (min_diversity = 1 turns this off).\n\nAn image of fewer than three pixels has no region (VLFeat returns none): MserRegion[], the area fractions not checked.\n\ndark_on_bright returns the dark regions (polarity = :dark) and bright_on_dark the light ones (:light), the components of img .>= l: VLFeat runs the same on 255 .- img, whose <= level sets these are. Dark regions come first; each polarity is ordered as VLFeat orders it, by level (darkest first, resp. brightest first), then by seed.\n\nInput\n\nA matrix of UInt8, N0f8 or Gray{N0f8}: VLFeat's vl_mser_pix is a byte, and these hold its 256 levels exactly. Any other element type is an ArgumentError: quantize explicitly (e.g. gray_u8), so the levels the regions are cut at are the caller's choice.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures: boundary_contours\n\njulia> img = fill(0xc8, 40, 60); img[11:20, 21:35] .= 0x28;   # a dark rectangle\n\njulia> r = only(detect_mser(img; bright_on_dark = false));\n\njulia> r.polarity, r.area, Tuple.(only(boundary_contours(r, img)))\n(:dark, 150, [(20.0, 10.0), (35.0, 10.0), (35.0, 20.0), (20.0, 20.0)])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detect_mser"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.region_mask-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.MserRegion, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Detection","text":"region_mask(region::MserRegion, img) -> BitMatrix\n\nThe pixels of region, an MSER of img, as a mask the size of img: the 8-connected component of img .<= region.level (:dark; .>= for :light) holding region.seed, filled from the seed as VLFeat's vl_erfill fills it. findall of it lists the pixels in ascending linear order. img must be the image the region was detected in: a seed outside it, a level other than img[seed] or a fill of another area than region.area is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.region_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detect_foerstner-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Detection","text":"detect_foerstner([T = Float32], img; device = default_device(),\n                 method = :foerstner, noise_sigma = 2.0,\n                 derivative = :gaussian2d, integration = :box,\n                 derivative_sigma = 1.0, integration_sigma = 1.41 * derivative_sigma,\n                 precision_threshold = 0.5, roundness_threshold = 0.3,\n                 significance_level = 0.999) -> StructArray{<:Detection}\n\nThe Förstner interest operator (Förstner and Gülch, 1987): the junction (corner) points and circular points of a grey image at sub-pixel precision, each with its covariance, after the reference implementation ip_fop.m (Luxen, 2005), with Köthe's doubled resolution and window criterion (Köthe, 2003).\n\nReturns one row per optimal window, a StructArray of Detection(Uncertain(PointFrame(x), Σ), (; label, window, F)) (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's PointFrame: a position, no scale):\n\nlabel: :corner (a junction point), :circular (Förstner & Gülch's circular point: the centre of a rotationally symmetric pattern, with no size estimate) or :unknown (the significance test assigned neither);\nthe frame: the corner or circular point's position and its 2×2 covariance (px², (x, y) order); for an :unknown row the window centre with a NaN covariance (the reference returns no estimate for it);\nwindow: the window centre, a Point2;\nF: the classification statistic Ω_corner / Ω_circular.\n\nfilter(d -> d.label === :corner, points) are the corners; Ellipse(d; sigma = 1) is a point's 1σ error ellipse (ip_errell.m's default).\n\nimg is any grey image the package's detectors take, read through gray_f32 as grey values in [0, 1] (an 8-bit image as value/255, as MATLAB's im2double); it is not stretched to its own range. The image-wide steps run on device, any KernelAbstractions backend, in precision T (the reference computes in Float64; supply Float64 for that).\n\nKeywords (defaults: the values the reference's CODE runs with)\n\nmethod: :foerstner — windows where the roundness 4 det/tr² > roundness_threshold and the precision det/tr > (σₙ / precision_threshold)², ranked by det/tr; :koethe — windows whose precision test succeeds, ranked by the smaller eigenvalue.\nnoise_sigma: the standard deviation of the (constant) image noise, in GREY LEVELS OF AN 8-BIT IMAGE (0–255), as the reference's for a uint8 image; σₙ = noise_sigma / 255 on the [0, 1] image.\nderivative: :gaussian2d (a Gaussian derivative smoothed across by a Gaussian of the same σ) or :gaussian1d (the derivative alone).\nintegration: :box (a box whose standard deviation is integration_sigma) or :gaussian.\nderivative_sigma, integration_sigma: in input pixels.\nprecision_threshold: the largest accepted point standard deviation, input px.\nroundness_threshold: the smallest accepted roundness (:foerstner only).\nsignificance_level: of the F-test that labels a point corner or circular.\n\nAlgorithm (ip_fop.m, step by step)\n\nThe image is doubled bilinearly (the package's one 2× upsampling, ASIFT's) and every scale and threshold doubled with it (lines 170–180).\nThe gradient by correlation with the derivative taps (radius ⌈3.3σ⌉, zero padding, as imfilter), its squares and product (the squared gradient), and their integration by the box (width 2⌈√(12σ² + 1)/2⌉ + 1) or the Gaussian (radius ⌈3.3σ⌉), zero padded (lines 184–207, 617–678).\nThe window measure (det/tr or the smaller eigenvalue) masked by the tests above; its regional maxima (8-connected plateaus higher than every neighbour, imregionalmax), thinned by a second non-maximum suppression over a square of radius 2⌈√(12σᵢ² + 1)⌉ doubled pixels, away from the border (lines 210–281).\nIn the (2ρ + 1)² window around each centre, ρ = ⌈√(12σᵢ² + 1)/2⌉ + 1 (σᵢ doubled), the pixels weighed equally (the reference's choice, also for a Gaussian integration): the corner is the least-squares intersection of the lines through every pixel ACROSS its gradient (normal matrix Σ ∇g∇gᵀ), the circular point that of the lines ALONG it (Σ ∇g⊥∇g⊥ᵀ). Each has a weighted residual sum Ω, redundancy (2ρ + 1)² − 2 and covariance Ω/redundancy · N⁻¹ (lines 314–423, 440, 449).\nF = Ω_corner / Ω_circular against the F distribution of (redundancy, redundancy) degrees of freedom, as the reference's (T < F(α)) − (T > F(1 − α)) (line 429): a corner below the 1 − significance_level quantile, a circular point above the significance_level quantile, unknown in between.\n\nCoordinates\n\nCOLMAP pixel centres of the input image, (x, y) = (column, row), converted once, at the output. The doubled image's pixel (r′, c′) (1-based) samples the input at 1-based (r′/2, c′/2), so a doubled-grid position is at COLMAP ((c′ − 1)/2, (r′ − 1)/2) in the input, and its covariance is the doubled one /4, reordered to (x, y).\n\nDifferences from ip_fop.m\n\nUndoing the doubling — fixed. The reference doubles with MATLAB's imresize(g, 2, 'bilinear') (line 172; doubled pixel j samples input j/2 + 1/4) but maps positions back with transf_H, transf_v (lines 340–350, applied at 436, 446, 457), the inverse of an align-corners grid: its points are off by ((n + 1)/2 − x)/(2n − 1) (1-based, n the image size), 0 at the centre, up to 0.25 px toward the centre at the borders. Here the doubling is the package's upsampling (input j/2, a quarter pixel from imresize's grid) undone by its own exact inverse.\nThe circular point's residual sum — kept as the reference has it. Its line 423 takes two of the three terms from the corner model's residuals; the circular model's own residual sum would take all three from its own. Monte Carlo coverage favours the reference's form, so it is kept.\nDefaults — the code's, not the header's. The header states SIGMA_N = 2.5 and SIGMA_DIFF = 0.7 (lines 37, 50); the code runs 2.0 and 1.0 (lines 521, 524). A documentation conflict, not a fix.\n\nThe docs page gives the evidence for each. The reference warns that with a Gaussian integration the window estimation still weighs the pixels equally; so does this.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures, VisualGeometryToolkitCore\n\njulia> img = [Float32((c - 20.5)^2 + (r - 24.5)^2 < 6.25) for r in 1:48, c in 1:40];   # a dot at pixel (24.5, 20.5)\n\njulia> points = detect_foerstner(img);\n\njulia> only(points).label\n:circular\n\njulia> round.(Tuple(only(points).center); digits = 1)   # COLMAP: (column − ½, row − ½)\n(20.0, 24.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.detect_foerstner"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.box_downsample-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Real, 3}, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"box_downsample(img::AbstractArray{<:Real, 3}, f::Integer) -> Array{Float64, 3}\n\nThe H × W × C image averaged over f × f blocks, per channel: output pixel (i, j) is the mean of input rows (i − 1)f + 1 : if and columns (j − 1)f + 1 : jf. The output is H ÷ f × W ÷ f × C; rows and columns past the last whole block are dropped. f = 1 returns a Float64 copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.box_downsample"},{"category":"function","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gaussian_blur","page":"Detection","text":"gaussian_blur(img::AbstractArray{<:Real, 3}, σ[, mask]) -> Array{Float64, 3}\n\nThe H × W × C image convolved per channel with the separable Gaussian of standard deviation σ pixels, truncated at ⌈3σ⌉ pixels and normalized to unit sum; at the image border the kernel is renormalized over the pixels inside it (no padding value enters). The taps are the package's one Gaussian taps builder's, at truncation 3 — not ASIFT's pyramid's 4σ with a mirrored border: the vignetting chain this smooths for was reproduced with this truncation and border rule, so they stay as they are.\n\nWith a mask (an H × W array of weights, 0 outside the support — a fisheye's image circle) it is the NORMALIZED convolution blur(img·mask) / blur(mask): pixels outside the mask do not bleed in, and a pixel where the mask is zero is 0. σ = 0 returns a Float64 copy (masked to zero outside the mask). Edge-safe smoothing of a linear image before it is sampled (the rooftop vignetting chain's cell samples).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gaussian_blur"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_linear_image-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Detection","text":"load_linear_image(path; srgb = false, downsample = 1) -> Array{Float64, 3}\n\nA PNG file as an H × W × 3 Float64 array of linear RGB values in [0, 1]: the samples AS STORED (read with gamma = 1, so a gAMA/sRGB chunk never re-encodes them; gray files are replicated to three channels, an alpha channel is dropped, 8- and 16-bit depths both normalized to [0, 1]).\n\nsrgb = true: the file is display-encoded (a phone or camera photograph); srgb_to_linear decodes every sample.\ndownsample = f: the decoded image averaged over f × f blocks (box_downsample), in linear space.\n\nThe array is indexed [row, column, channel]; pixel (row, column) is the image's PixelFrame{:julia} pixel (column, row).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.load_linear_image"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.srgb_to_linear-Tuple{Real}","page":"Detection","text":"srgb_to_linear(x) -> linear value\n\nThe sRGB (IEC 61966-2-1) electro-optical transfer function: an encoded value x ∈ [0, 1] to linear, x / 12.92 for x ≤ 0.04045, otherwise ((x + 0.055) / 1.055)^2.4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.srgb_to_linear"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.batched_rows-Tuple{Any, Int64, Int64}","page":"Detection","text":"batched_rows(run_chunk, N::Int, max_batch::Int; retry_single = false) -> Matrix{Float32}\n\nRun run_chunk(offset, n) over 1:N in chunks of at most max_batch rows (max_batch <= 0 means one chunk) and assemble the N × D result: the row width D is read off the FIRST chunk's output (any shape with n * D elements), the matrix is allocated lazily then, and each chunk is assigned into its contiguous row slice. With retry_single, a chunk that throws is retried one row at a time (the batch size drops to 1 for the rest of the run) — the recovery an inference session wants when a batch overflows its arena. N == 0 returns an empty 0 × 0 matrix.\n\nHoisted from BlobBoards, where the ONNX host, ORT device-tensor and two Lux describe backends had each drifted their own copy (2026-09).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.batched_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detect(img; device=default_device(), nms=:mxm, window_half=3,\n             cutoff=3.0, intensity_thr=0.04, edge_thr=10.0,\n             polarity=:none, band_octaves=0.0, band_reach=1, boundary_K=3.0,\n             tilts=nothing, upsample=true, seed_sigma=1.6, raw=false,\n             uncertainty=nothing)\n\nRun the IMAS-25 ASIFT detector on img (H×W; Gray, UInt8, or real-valued in [0,1]) and return Vector{Detection{EllipticFrame}} in COLMAP coords.\n\nCoordinates. A detection's center is (x, y), with x along the image columns and y along the rows, in COLMAP pixel-centre coordinates: the top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5), so a blob centred on the pixel img[r, c] is returned at (c − 0.5, r − 0.5). Measured on isotropic Gaussian blobs on the CPU (pinned by the test suite):\n\na blob centred on a pixel centre comes back exact (within 0.005 px);\nbetween pixel centres, the quadratic sub-pixel refinement pulls the centre toward the nearest pixel centre, by at most 0.03 px at σ = 4 (−0.007, −0.015, −0.021, −0.027 px at fractions 0.1–0.4, mirrored above 0.5) and 0.06 px for σ from 2 to 8;\nan exact half-pixel centre, where the DoG ties between the two pixels, is refined like any other: the extremum test breaks the tie toward the first pixel, so it carries that side's bias (−0.031 px at σ = 4). A test strict against all 26 neighbours would reject both pixels and leave an unrefined detection from a coarser octave, 0.5 px low;\na feature found only on a pyramid boundary scale slice is not refined and sits on that octave's pixel grid.\n\nWith raw = true the features are in 0-indexed OpenCV coordinates (top-left pixel centre (0, 0)), 0.5 less on both axes.\n\nnms selects the cross-warp non-max suppression: :mxm (centroid window, default), :ellipse (Mahalanobis footprint), or :none (full per-warp feature pool). polarity gates by DoG sign (:dark → response > 0, :light → response < 0, :none → both) BEFORE the cross-warp NMS, so a blob of the other sign never suppresses a kept one. band_octaves > 0 makes the NMS scale-banded: a feature can only be suppressed by features whose geometric-mean scale band ⌊log2(√det S) / band_octaves⌋ is within band_reach of its own, so board-sized extrema and tilted-warp streaks stop erasing the small blobs inside them (see asift_detect!). raw=true returns the ImasFeatureKA vector instead of Detections. device, tilts, upsample and seed_sigma pick the plan; the others but raw go to asift_detect!, which refuses a keyword it does not take.\n\nKeypoint uncertainty. uncertainty = FeatureCovariance() returns each detection's frame as Uncertain(frame, Σ̃), Σ̃ = cov(d) a 3×3 CovMat over (x, y, log σ) for unit iid pixel noise in the detector's intensity units — see FeatureCovariance for the model, the parameterization and the units. With raw = true the result is then (; features, cov). The default nothing runs the detector without it, the kernels compiled exactly as before.\n\nThe plan for (size, device, tilts, upsample, seed_sigma) is cached (see set_max_cached_plans!) and every call holds a process-wide lock, so concurrent calls are safe but do not run in parallel. On a KA.CPU() device every kernel launch is statically scheduled over the threads, or dynamically when the call runs inside a threaded region (e.g. a Threads.@threads loop over frames); the features are the same either way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detections-Tuple{Vector{ImasFeatureKA}}","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detections(feats::Vector{ImasFeatureKA}) -> Vector{Detection}\nasift_detections(feats, cov) -> Vector{Detection}\n\nConvert raw ASIFT features (0-indexed opencv pixel coords, input frame) into Detection{EllipticFrame}s in COLMAP convention (+0.5 on both axes, so the top-left pixel's centre is (0.5, 0.5); see asift_detect). The metadata is (response, edge_score, laplacian_scale_score, warp_idx): laplacian_scale_score = sign(response) (+1 dark, −1 light — the DoG sign convention polarity gates on), which is what is_dark and is_light read; edge_score is a placeholder (always 0). Given the features' covariances (asift_detect!(…; uncertainty = FeatureCovariance())), each frame is Uncertain(frame, Σ̃) (see FeatureCovariance); the +0.5 shift leaves a covariance unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detections"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.clear_asift_plans!-Tuple{}","page":"Detection","text":"clear_asift_plans!()\n\nDrop all cached AsiftPlans, releasing their device buffers immediately rather than leaving them to a finalizer.\n\nThe cache is keyed by (W, H, device, tilts, upsample, seed_sigma) and holds at most set_max_cached_plans! plans (default 1), evicting the least recently used. Call this to release the resident plans outright — e.g. between sequences in a mixed-resolution corpus, or before handing the device to another workload.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.clear_asift_plans!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.set_max_cached_plans!-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"set_max_cached_plans!(n) -> Int\n\nSet how many AsiftPlans stay resident (default 1). Evicted plans have their device buffers released immediately via KernelAbstractions.unsafe_free!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.set_max_cached_plans!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.AsiftPlan","page":"Detection","text":"AsiftPlan(W, H; device = default_device(), tilts = <IMAS-25>, seed_sigma = 1.6,\n          input_blur = 0.5, nb_scales = 3, capacity = 2^20, chunk = 0,\n          upsample = true)\n\nThe preallocated buffers of the ASIFT detector for W×H images on device (any KernelAbstractions backend): the tilted warps, the scale-space pyramids, the feature pool and the compiled kernels. asift_detect! runs the detector in them without allocating on the device.\n\nasift_detect builds and caches plans itself (see set_max_cached_plans! and clear_asift_plans!), so most callers never construct one. Build one to own its lifetime, or to detect in a loop over same-size frames without the cache's lock.\n\ntilts: the tilt schedule, a vector of (t, φ) pairs (tilt and longitude in radians). The default is the 25 tilts of IMAS-25; [(1.0, 0.0)] alone is plain DoG-SIFT.\nseed_sigma, input_blur, nb_scales, upsample: the SIFT pyramid's seed blur, the input's assumed blur, the scales per octave and whether the first octave is the 2× upsampled image.\ncapacity: the initial size of the feature pool; it grows when a frame produces more extrema.\nchunk: how many warps stay resident at once; 0 sizes the chunk from the device's free memory.\n\nA plan holds device memory (several GiB for a large image under the full schedule). It is mutable only because its feature pool grows.\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> plan = AsiftPlan(64, 48; tilts = [(1.0, 0.0)]);\n\njulia> img = zeros(Float32, 64, 48);   # (W, H): x along the first axis\n\njulia> asift_detect!(plan, img)\nImasFeatureKA[]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.AsiftPlan"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._chunk_warps-Tuple{Any, Int64, Int64, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"_chunk_warps(dev, nw, warp_bytes, chunk) -> Int\n\nWarps to keep resident per chunk. chunk > 0 is honoured verbatim (capped at nw); chunk <= 0 auto-sizes from device_free_bytes so the pyramids fit in _PYRAMID_BUDGET of what is free right now.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._chunk_warps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect!-Tuple{AsiftPlan, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"Detection","text":"asift_detect!(plan, img_xy; nms=:none, window_half=3, cutoff=3.0,\n              polarity=:none, band_octaves=0.0, band_reach=1,\n              intensity_thr=0.04, edge_thr=10.0,\n              boundary_K=3.0, uncertainty=nothing) -> Vector{ImasFeatureKA}\n\nFull batched IMAS-25 detection in the plan's preallocated buffers. With nms = :mxm or :ellipse the cross-warp NMS runs on-device and only the survivors are returned; :none returns the full boundary-filtered pool. polarity (:dark → response > 0, :light → response < 0) gates the pool BEFORE the NMS splat, so a blob of the other sign can never suppress a kept one. band_octaves > 0 restricts suppression to features of comparable scale: a feature in scale band b = ⌊log2(√det S) / band_octaves⌋ can only be suppressed by features in bands b ± band_reach (see _band_pool!); 0 is the whole-pool rule. The seed blur σ₀ — the pyramid's, and the one the reported σ is scaled by — is the plan's (AsiftPlan(W, H; seed_sigma)).\n\nuncertainty = FeatureCovariance() also returns each feature's covariance: the result is then (; features, cov), cov[i] the FeatureCovariance covariance of features[i], a CovMat{3} over (x, y, log σ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.asift_detect!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.device_free_bytes-Tuple{Any}","page":"Detection","text":"device_free_bytes(dev) -> Int\n\nFree memory on dev, in bytes, used to size the tilt chunk and the matcher's score blocks. The package that defines the device's backend type answers: for CUDA that is free_memory() of CUDA (5.x) or CUDACore (6.x). A backend with no known query gets typemax(Int), which disables chunking, with a one-time warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.device_free_bytes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.ImasFeatureKA","page":"Detection","text":"ImasFeatureKA\n\nOne raw feature of the ASIFT detector, as asift_detect! and asift_detect(img; raw = true) return it, before conversion to a Detection by asift_detections. All fields are in the input image's frame:\n\nx, y: the refined centre in 0-indexed OpenCV pixel coordinates (top-left pixel centre at (0, 0), 0.5 less than the COLMAP coordinates of a Detection);\nσ: the characteristic scale, in input pixels;\nintensity: the DoG response at the extremum, positive for a dark blob and negative for a light one;\ns_xx, s_xy, s_yy: the symmetric 2×2 shape matrix of the elliptic frame, back-projected from the tilted warp it was found in;\nwarp_idx: the index of that warp in the plan's tilt schedule (1 is the identity).\n\njulia> using VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\n\njulia> f = ImasFeatureKA(10.0, 20.0, 2.0, -0.1, 2.0, 0.0, 2.0, 1);\n\njulia> d = only(asift_detections([f]));\n\njulia> d.center, is_light(d)\n([10.5, 20.5], true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.ImasFeatureKA"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.FeatureCovariance","page":"Detection","text":"FeatureCovariance()\n\nAsk asift_detect for each keypoint's 3×3 covariance over (x, y, log σ), by the implicit function theorem, for iid unit pixel noise.\n\nasift_detect(img; uncertainty = FeatureCovariance()) returns detections whose frame is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Uncertain(frame, Σ̃); the default uncertainty = nothing runs the detector unchanged. cov(d) is Σ̃, feature_frame(d) the frame, and d.center, d.σ and the metadata read as on any detection.\n\nModel. A refined DoG keypoint is a stationary point of the detector's local quadratic model of the DoG in (slice, x, y). With Ĥ its finite-difference Hessian and C the covariance, under unit iid pixel noise, of that model's gradient at the keypoint, Σ̃ = J·Ĥ⁻¹·C·Ĥ⁻¹·Jᵀ: C is tabulated exactly for the discrete pyramid (warp, upsample, blurs, octave downsamples) once per plan, and J maps to the reported coordinates, then through the ASIFT back-projection.\n\nParameterization. Σ̃ is a CovMat{3} over (x, y, log σ) in the detection's image frame (COLMAP pixel centres, as d.center), σ = d.σ (the frame's characteristic scale √det S), log natural. Position-only consumers take the top-left 2×2 block (the marginal). A tilted ASIFT warp's frame has the shape S = σ_w·M_w with M_w fixed by the warp (d.warp_idx into the tilt schedule), so (x, y, log σ) are its only free parameters and Σ̃ is full rank.\n\nUnits. Unit noise variance in the detector's intensity units: those of a float input image, and the [0, 1] scale for 8-bit input (UInt8, N0f8, …; σn = σgray / 255). The physical covariance is σ_n²·Σ̃.\n\nUnrefined detections — on a boundary scale slice, or whose sub-pixel refinement failed — sit on the octave's pixel grid and have no such covariance: their Σ̃ is all NaN.\n\nExtension point (not implemented): an intensity-dependent noise model, e.g. FeatureCovariance(noise = ShotNoise(a, b)), makes the middle term K·diag(σ²(I))·Kᵀ, gathered from the pyramid per keypoint instead of tabulated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.FeatureCovariance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._ift_cov_reference-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, Any, AbstractMatrix, Real, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"_ift_cov_reference(H, off, M, sf, S) -> SMatrix{3, 3} over (x, y, log σ)\n\nHost reference of the covariance _bextract! emits, through VisualGeometryToolkitCore's implicit_covariance: the square system g(v, f) = ĝ + Ĥ·(v − v₀) = 0 with A = ∂g/∂v = Ĥ and B = ∂g/∂f = P(off) over the nine functionals f, whose covariance is M; then propagate by J = diag(sf, sf, ln2/S), which also reorders (s, x, y) to (x, y, log σ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures._ift_cov_reference"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gaussian_taps-Tuple{Real}","page":"Detection","text":"gaussian_taps(sep_sigma) -> Vector{Float32}\n\nOne-sided normalized Gaussian taps [k0, k1, …, kr] (center + r taps), with r = max(1, ceil(4·sep_sigma)). Normalized so k0 + 2·Σ kᵢ = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.gaussian_taps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.sift_nb_octaves-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Detection","text":"sift_nb_octaves(W, H; upsample=false) -> Int\n\nAuto octave count: floor(log2(min(W,H))) − 4 (+1 if upsampling).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.sift_nb_octaves"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.sift_sep_sigmas-Tuple{Real, Real, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"sift_sep_sigmas(seed_sigma, input_blur, nb_scales) -> Vector{Float32}\n\nPer-level separable-blur σ for one octave (nbscales+3 levels). Level 0 lifts the assumed `inputblurup toseedsigma`; each subsequent level advances by a factor 2^(1/nbscales) in absolute scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.sift_sep_sigmas"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.imas_rotated_canvas-Tuple{Integer, Integer, Real}","page":"Detection","text":"Rotated-canvas geometry for a θ rotation of an nx×ny image (Float32, matches C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.imas_rotated_canvas"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detection/#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cross_warp_nms-Tuple{AbstractVector{ImasFeatureKA}, Integer, Integer}","page":"Detection","text":"cross_warp_nms(feats, W, H; device, mode=:mxm, window_half=3, cutoff=3.0,\n               band_octaves=0.0, band_reach=1) -> Vector{ImasFeatureKA}\n\nCross-warp NMS over an aggregated feature pool (input-frame). mode is :mxm (centroid window window_half) or :ellipse (Mahalanobis footprint, cutoff cutoff). band_octaves > 0 restricts suppression to features of comparable scale — band ⌊log2(√det S) / band_octaves⌋ ± band_reach. It runs the kernels asift_detect! runs on its device pool, so on the pool asift_detect(img; nms = :none, raw = true) returns it keeps the features asift_detect(img; nms = mode, raw = true) keeps, given the same detection keywords — up to exact ties in the quantized response, which break toward the later pool index (and pool order varies between calls on a multithreaded or GPU backend). Returns the survivors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.cross_warp_nms"},{"category":"section","location":"#VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.jl","page":"Home","text":"(Image: CI) (Image: Docs: stable) (Image: Docs: dev) (Image: Coverage)\n\nFeature detection, description and matching for the VisualGeometry stack.\n\nDetection. ASIFT (IMAS-25) on KernelAbstractions. asift_detect runs on the CPU or any KernelAbstractions GPU backend and returns Detections (a feature frame plus detector metadata), optionally with each keypoint's covariance. VisualGeometryToolkitIO writes and reads detections as JSON.\nMatching. Nearest-neighbour descriptor matching, with one KernelAbstractions implementation for the CPU and every GPU backend (nn_match, match), and a stacked matcher over many galleries (nn_match_stacked).\nLoMa. The learned DeDoDe descriptors and LoMa matchers of LoMa (Nordström et al., ECCV 2026), run from the authors' ONNX exports through ONNX Runtime on the CPU or CUDA (LoMaDescriptor, LoMaMatcher). See Citing.\nExtensions. JLD2 backs FileCache, and Makie draws detections and correspondences.\n\nThe geometric types (feature frames, correspondences, covariances) are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's. This package adds the detector, the descriptors and the matchers that work on them.","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\")\n\nThe package requires Julia 1.12 and is part of the VisualGeometryToolkit stack.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"The example detects the blobs of a synthetic image and matches two sets of descriptors. It needs no files and no network.\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures\nusing Random   # a standard library, shipped with Julia\n\n# A 120×160 gray image with three Gaussian blobs, given as\n# (row, column, σ, amplitude): two light blobs and one dark one.\nblobs = [(40, 50, 4.0, 0.35), (70, 100, 3.0, -0.3), (30, 130, 6.0, 0.3)]\nimg = [0.5 + sum(a * exp(-((r - y)^2 + (c - x)^2) / (2s^2)) for (y, x, s, a) in blobs)\n       for r in 1:120, c in 1:160]\n\n# Detect with the default IMAS-25 tilt schedule.\ndets = asift_detect(img)\nlength(dets) == 3                      # true\ncount(is_dark, dets) == 1              # true\n\n# A centre is (x, y) in COLMAP pixel coordinates, where the top-left pixel's\n# centre is (0.5, 0.5): the blob at row 70, column 100 is found at (99.5, 69.5).\ndark = only(filter(is_dark, dets))\nround.(dark.center; digits = 1) == [99.5, 69.5]   # true\n\n# Match descriptors, one per row. Here they are random unit vectors, and the\n# second set is the first shuffled and perturbed.\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nunit_rows(D) = D ./ sqrt.(sum(abs2, D; dims = 2))\nD1 = unit_rows(randn(rng, Float32, 100, 32))\nperm = randperm(rng, 100)\nD2 = unit_rows(D1[perm, :] .+ 0.05f0 .* randn(rng, Float32, 100, 32))\nmatches = nn_match(D1, D2)             # mutual nearest neighbours, cosine similarity\nall(perm[m.second] == m.first for m in matches)   # true\n\nDetections are written to and read from JSON by VisualGeometryToolkitIO (json_value(d, contract; kind), from_json(Detection, obj, contract; kind)), which owns the stack's quantity JSON format.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Getting started: the quick start, step by step.\nDetection: the ASIFT detector, its coordinates, accuracy and keypoint covariance.\nMatching: strategies, metrics, stacked galleries and LoMa.\nAPI reference.\n\nThe stack's shared conventions (function names, argument order, coordinate frames and pixel origins) are documented in VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Conventions.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitFeatures is part of VisualGeometryToolkit. Cite the toolkit; its CITATION.cff is the source of this entry (GitHub's \"Cite this repository\" button exports it):\n\n@software{pritts2026vgtk,\n  author = {Pritts, James and Sauer, Hendrik},\n  title  = {{VisualGeometryToolkit.jl}: Julia Packages for Multiple-View Geometry},\n  url    = {https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/VisualGeometryToolkit.jl},\n  year   = {2026}\n}\n\nCite also the papers behind the methods you use, listed on the portal.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"section","location":"#LoMa","page":"Home","text":"LoMaDescriptor and LoMaMatcher run the networks of LoMa by David Nordström, Johan Edstedt, Georg Bökman, Jonathan Astermark, Anders Heyden, Viktor Larsson, Mårten Wadenbäck, Michael Felsberg and Fredrik Kahl. The architectures, the trained weights and the ONNX exports are theirs, downloaded from the authors' release; this package only feeds them keypoints and reads their matches back. The descriptors are DeDoDe's (Edstedt et al.). If you use them in published work, cite both:\n\n@inproceedings{nordstrom2026loma,\n  title     = {{LoMa: Local Feature Matching Revisited}},\n  author    = {David Nordström and Johan Edstedt and Georg Bökman and Jonathan Astermark and Anders Heyden and Viktor Larsson and Mårten Wadenbäck and Michael Felsberg and Fredrik Kahl},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  year      = {2026}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{edstedt2024dedode,\n  title     = {{DeDoDe: Detect, Don't Describe --- Describe, Don't Detect for Local Feature Matching}},\n  author    = {Johan Edstedt and Georg Bökman and Mårten Wadenbäck and Michael Felsberg},\n  booktitle = {2024 International Conference on 3D Vision (3DV)},\n  year      = {2024},\n  organization = {IEEE}\n}\n\nLoMa's code is MIT-licensed except its matcher, which inherits LightGlue's Apache-2.0; DeDoDe is MIT except the DINOv2 backbone of DeDoDe-G (Apache-2.0).","title":"LoMa"},{"category":"section","location":"#License","page":"Home","text":"MIT","title":"License"}]
}
